WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
"Fear of terrorism is widespread in America."

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.900
"Many Americans have actually changed how they live their daily lives."

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.900
"A significant number of Americans say they avoid sporting events, concerts,"

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.900
"and other public events because of their fear of terrorism."

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.500
Let me ask you a question: what are people most afraid of?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.500
You may think it's insects, or natural disasters, or even public speaking,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.000
but an extensive survey conducted by Chapman University in 2015 said

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
the biggest fears in Americans are cited as the, quote: "man-made disasters",

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.900
These fears include bio-warfare, terrorism, and nuclear attacks.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.800
44% of Americans are afraid or very afraid of terrorist attacks.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.300
Compare that to 28% for public speaking and 25% for insects.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.400
Fear of terrorism actually peaked following 9/11

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.600
as the majority of Americans were worried that they, or someone they knew,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.600
would be a victim of a terrorist attack.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.400
I wanna suggest that what the public fears is mirrored in Hollywood cinema,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.300
especially the zombie movies directly following 9/11.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.800
But to start, we have to go back in time

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.900
to the horror movies of the 1950's.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.600
So let me rephrase the first question:

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.500
what were people most afraid of in, say, 1951?

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.500
*50's PSA narrator*: "We all know the atomic bomb is very dangerous."

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.600
*cont.*: "Since it may be used against us, we must get ready for it."

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.800
With the Korean War, Soviet nuclear testing, and McCarthy-ism in full force,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.000
a 1950 poll showed an astounding 68% of Americans thought the Soviet Union

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.900
would use an atomic weapon against the United States.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.900
This fear was even used later as a scare-tactic in an infamous campaign ad by Lyndon Johnson,

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.800
showing just how powerful the fear was on the public.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.200
*campaign ad narrator*: "These are the stakes."

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:38.000
*cont.*: "To make a world in which ALL of God's children can live, or to go into the dark."

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.300
*cont.*: "We must either love each other, or we must die."

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.500
So how did horror movies reflect this fear?

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.600
The 1950's brought on a wave of disaster movies where aliens or monsters attack,

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.700
like 'War of the Worlds' , 'The Blob', and 'The Thing From Another World'.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.200
When you watch battle scenes from the original 'War of the Worlds'

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.000
or 'Attack of the 50 ft. Woman' or 'Godzilla',

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.400
You notice the parallels between these horror movies and the destructive nature of the atomic bomb.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:08.400
In fact, the director of 'Godzilla' was very blunt about his intentions with the now iconic monster.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.500
He said, quote:

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.000
 

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
For Americans, however, the fear was channeled towards the Soviet Union,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.900
our biggest global threat at the time.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.200
In the same way that Godzilla stomps on buildings, and is immune to bullets,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.500
so too would an atomic bomb wipe out cities and be entirely unstoppable by us.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.800
Maybe it's no coincidence that the Japanese film 'Godzilla' was such a huge success in America.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.000
The Japanese fear of the bomb was just as relevant to Americans at the time.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.900
Now, let's jump to the 21st century,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.900
We're no longer threatened by a huge, visible, global superpower.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.100
Actually, the fear of terrorism is quite the opposite.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.600
It's nothing like Godzilla, a giant monster who drops in on a city.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.400
A terrorist hides in the shadows, disguises themselves as an everyday citizen,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.000
and causes society to collapse from the inside-out.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.100
So here's my point:

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.000
doesn't the method of destruction for a terrorist sound a lot like the method of destruction for a zombie?

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.900
First, it's important to note the explosion of the zombie genre.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.700
io9 posted a nice graph showing a boom in zombie movies in the early 2000's,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.900
paralleling with mainstream video games like 'Resident Evil' and 'Call of Duty' ,

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.900
and spilling into other media like the TV show 'The Walking Dead',

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.600
there's no doubt that zombies have infected the public conscious.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:21.800
'28 Days Later' is one of the first successful zombie movies to come out following 9/11,

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.700
and it highlights contemporary fears of terroism and decay of social order.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.000
The scene that most strongly ties the movie to terrorist fears is actually the very first scene.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.300
The deadly virus is created in a lab by making chimpanzees watch footage

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.000
of Arabic mobs fighting police, burning cars, and beating each other up.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.900
This virus is literally created from Arabic violence.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.200
I particularly like this shot, which makes the human look like it's regressing back into a terrifying animal.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.000
An important element of the zombie genre is that the enemy comes inside a community

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.900
and spreads from person to person.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.700
This sounds eerily similar to the method of attack for a terrorist.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.200
A sleeper cell hides within our borders ready to strike,

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.900
and once we can finally spot it, it's too late to stop.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.300
I think another key difference between 50's monster movies and zombie movies

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.000
is the role of the government.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.500
In monster movies, the government often fights against the enemy,

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.700
but in '28 Days Later', the government has abandoned them.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:11.800
"What about the government? What are they doing?"

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.000
"There's no government."

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.000
"Of course there's a government!"

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.000
"There's always a government, they're in a, a bunker, or, or a plane!"

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.800
"No, there's no government. No police."

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Not only that, but in the movie the soldiers actually fight against the protagonist.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.100
In the 21st century, with the controversial invasion of Iraq and government surveillance,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.900
terrorism has brought on a distrust of our own government.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.600
Where the 1950's government was always joining in the fight against a common enemy,

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.700
it appears that during a zombie apocalypse, we shouldn't count on any government to protect us.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.000
What's so special about cinema, is that it's such a democratic art form.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.200
When it comes to art forms like painting, music, or creative writing,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.400
the message is only channeled through one person, but with cinema,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.800
the final work is the product of dozens of people.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.100
Not only that, but a production process as expensive as making a movie

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.400
requires that mainstream movies appeal to as many people as possible.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.000
As a result, movies tend to be a window into the general mindset of the society it was made in.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.800
This means that the subject matter of a successful horror movie has, in it's subtext,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.600
a reflection of the general fears of the environment it was made in.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.700
There are things that, regardless of time period and society, humans are scared of:

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.900
the dark is a universal fear because we can't identify the threat.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.500
So, a movie like 'Don't Breathe' seems to do well because,

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.700
like 'Jaws', and the ending of 'Silence of the Lambs',

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.000
it's scary when the threat can see you, but you can't see them.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.500
It's a timeless fear.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.700
But what about fears that do change over time?

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.800
I talked about monster movies versus zombie movies,

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.000
but I also think mainstream horror movies about religion are another perfect example.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.600
Religion is at an all-time low in the United States.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.100
More importantly, the fear of damnation is low,

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.900
meaning that religious groups tend to spend less time on the pain and torment of hell in their teachings,

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.400
and more time on the love and forgiveness that comes from salvation.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.700
This idea is especially true in the Catholic church,

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:58.100
who revised their catechism in 1994 to remove the literary fire and brimstone description of hell,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.600
in favor of a metaphorical place symbolizing the loss of one's faith.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.000
So, how is this idea shown in modern movies?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.300
Around 40 or so years ago, most movies about the supernatural centered around religion.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:13.900
The devil is often the center villain in successful movies like 'Rosemary's Baby', 'The Omen', and 'The Exorcist'.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.500
Nowadays, though, supernatural beings are still present, but religion is largely ignored.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:21.900
The ghost is a former mental patient or some unexplained being.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.300
'The Exorcist' is about a child being possessed by the devil.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.800
'Orphan' has a very similar premise, a child who is acting strangely, but religion is hardly even mentioned.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:33.200
The fact is, that people don't find the devil as scary as they used to,

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:39.300
partly due to less religion, and partly due to a conscious choice to focus less on damnation in religious doctrine.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:43.500
There's no doubt that there are detectable trends in what horror movies choose to find scary.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.300
If you wanna know what people are afraid of,

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.300
you could run an expensive and time consuming statistical study,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.900
or you could just look at what horror movies they find scary.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.900
So the next time someone says: "don't be scared, it's just a movie!", don't believe them.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:55.900
Thanks for watching.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:04.000
*Summertime by DJ Jazzy Jeff and The Fresh Prince plays*

